# Nightly backups for the podpack site, on the Opalstack host.
#
# Install with `crontab ops/backup.cron` (or paste into `crontab -e`) as the
# site's own user, and RE-INSTALL after editing this file -- crontab keeps its
# own copy and does not read this one.
#
# Cron runs with a minimal environment, and rootless podman needs three things
# from it that an interactive login shell supplies and cron does not. The first
# scheduled run found none of them: it reported both stores down while
# `podman compose ps` showed them Up 20 hours -- the containers were fine, cron
# simply could not see them, and backup.sh/verify-backup.sh rightly refused.
#
#   1. loginctl enable-linger (once, as this user): keeps /run/user/<uid> alive
#      while you are logged out -- both what lets the containers outlive logout
#      and what makes the rootless socket under it reachable at 3am.
#   2. $HOME/bin on PATH: `podman compose` shells out to the compose provider at
#      ~/bin/docker-compose, which cron's /usr/bin:/bin PATH does not include.
#   3. XDG_RUNTIME_DIR: where podman finds the rootless socket and its container
#      state. Unset, it looks elsewhere and sees nothing. If podman still cannot
#      connect, also export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS="unix:path=$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus".
#
# 2 and 3 are exported on each line below; 1 is a one-off you run by hand.
#
# The `date` header stamps each run with an ISO-8601 instant, so a failure says
# exactly WHEN it fired. Its percent signs are backslash-escaped because cron
# turns an unescaped % into a newline and sends the rest to stdin -- which would
# otherwise eat the timestamp silently.
#
# $HOME is set by cron, so no username is hard-coded. Paths are the STAGING
# host's (app `os`); for production, change `os` to that app's directory.
# backup.sh reads the site's .env, so SITE_NAME -- and therefore BACKUP_ROOT
# (~/backups/$SITE_NAME) -- comes from there; nothing to set here.
#
# Prove it once before trusting it: run backup.sh by hand, then restore.sh into
# a scratch deployment (operations.md sections 5 and 6). A backup nobody has
# restored is not yet a backup.

# 03:17 -- take the backup.
17 3 * * * export PATH="$HOME/bin:$PATH" XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="/run/user/$(id -u)"; cd $HOME/apps/os/site && { date -u +'---- \%FT\%TZ backup ----'; ./scripts/backup.sh; } >> $HOME/backups/backup.log 2>&1
# 04:23 -- read every archive back through the tool that would restore it; a
# non-zero exit (logged) is the thing to notice.
23 4 * * * export PATH="$HOME/bin:$PATH" XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="/run/user/$(id -u)"; cd $HOME/apps/os/site && { date -u +'---- \%FT\%TZ verify ----'; ./scripts/verify-backup.sh; } >> $HOME/backups/verify.log 2>&1
